chinese foreign minister wang yi has  made a surprise visit to india two years  after a deadly clash between troops from  both countries on their disputed border  wang yi met indian foreign minister s  jay shankar and national security  adviser ajit doval the visit comes in  the backdrop of a slump in bilateral  relations  a slump caused by a deadly clash between  troops of both nations in may 2020 india  accused china of breaking border  agreements and intruding into india's  claimed territory in the northern ladakh  region soldiers remain deployed along  the border and attempts to disengage  haven't yet been successful  here's a quick recap of events  a remote mountain region where  superpowers have faced off for decades  the yellow line marks the boundary  around 3 500 kilometers long between  india and china known as the line of  actual control or lac  it's not a hard border the two countries  have never agreed on where it should lie  tensions spiked in may 2020  both sides assemble troops in the area  amid reports that chinese soldiers had  set up camps on territory claimed by  india  china ordered its military to prepare  for quote worst case scenarios  a month later the two sides clashed in  the galwan valley  twenty indian soldiers were killed china  confirmed four dead a number that's been  disputed  india's prime minister responded  aggressively  beijing said it was defending its  territorial integrity accusing indian  troops of crossing the border and  provoking chinese soldiers  since the galwan incident chinese and  indian officials have met for several  rounds of talks aimed to de-escalate  tensions  in february 2021 they agreed to a  coordinated pullback  the indian military released footage of  chinese soldiers emptying out camps near  the disputed border and said it was  conducting similar actions  both sides claim the other is building  up defensive positions to fortify their  territorial claims  as mistrust continues between the  nuclear-armed rivals  and joining me now for more is jabin t  jacob he's one of india's foremost china  analysts and an associate professor at  the shiv nadar university near the  capital delhi mr jacob can i just begin  by asking you an utterly basic question  why did wang yi visit india now  well i think there are at least a couple  of reasons  one  what was reported in the news  first was that he was in town to invite  prime minister modi to the brics summit  in china  at the end of the year  but i think uh from reports that are  coming out uh  again just a few hours ago  uh  mr wangi also met with the external  affairs minister in india dr subramaniam  and they've had talks uh on the boundary  issue so at least uh  we know that these two issues uh were  discussed  uh so  given the state of uh affairs on the  line of actual control the disputed  boundary between india and china right  naturally  top of the agenda for the indians  speaking about that agenda  foreign improvement in bilateral  relations to an improvement in the  situation on that border that you were  talking about do you think wangi's visit  will help in that direction  i'm afraid i don't  believe that's likely to be the case  the chinese have given no indication  over two years that they are actually  interested in a quick solution to the  problem  we are yet to understand  what it is  that caused the chinese to engage in  these transgressions in the first place  the chinese violated decades worth of  bilateral agreements uh international  law  with their transgressions in the summer  of 2020.  so  we've had 15 rounds of military to  military talks  in the meantime and  progress as a  indian foreign minister just mentioned  has been less than desirable i mean at  the pace that is less than desirable and  i don't see that changing anytime soon  so what really is uh the the chinese  motivation here do they not want an  improvement in ties with india  uh i'm afraid the evidence points to  that direction uh the chinese are  currently at a stage where a  sense of exceptionalism a huge degree of  hyper-nationalism  drives domestic politics  and  there's a very black and white  ideological  conflict with the liberal democratic  order  that the communist party of china sees  and this conflict is is in a way good  for the existence  or continued  continuance of in power of the communist  party of china because  it  simplifies things uh for the domestic  audience it  gives the cpc the opportunity to say  look  uh the world outside uh is a bad place  we are in conflict with the world  outside and the cpc is best place to  take care of china's interests  and as long as that is a dominant  narrative at home  conflicts with the united states  with other democracies like india  will be seen as power for the course  give us a sense of the thinking in india  there have been at least 15 rounds of  military dialogue with the chinese with  no tangible result i mean troops are  still in a face-off on the border areas  does india need to accept the  possibility that china will not vacate  claimed indian territory where chinese  troops currently are  i think  india does uh at least  the smart minds within the government  system  do realize that the chinese are very  unlikely to walk away  from the areas that they've occupied  in 2020 um  but the  narrative on this side on the indian  side is that look  we've had situations like this before  uh for example in the late 1980s uh  there was a piece of territory in  arunachal pradesh that the chinese had  occupied and they took about eight years  to finally negotiate chinese withdrawal  from this area  so the indians  currently  i think labor under the impression that  this is likely to be the case also in  this instance  but uh i'm afraid that is not  uh going to be the case i mean the china  of the 1980s is not the china of today  today the chinese have a lot more  wealth economic wealth and military  might as well as national narrative  that posits uh these sort of conflicts  as essential  for china's what the chinese communist  party calls its national rejuvenation  so i think we are in a very different uh  sort of situation here  and the indian government makes a  mistake if it assumes  that it can  get the chinese to vacate only by talks  right we'll have to leave it there for  the time being but thank you so much for  joining us today jabinti jacob
 

